WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.960
So this sample may not look very much

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:13.809
but it's a sample of the saltiest water that you can find on this planet.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:20.800
It comes from a really small lake - not even a lake, a pond - in the north of Ethiopia.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:23.049
This was collected by one of my friends,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.869
Eduardo Perez, when he was on holiday

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:34.148
collected in a plastic water bottle and then he analyzed it and found that it was the saltiest water

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:39.308
that's ever been discovered, and he's got it into the Guinness World Records.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:42.759
Ethiopia, in case you've forgotten, is on the

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:49.869
eastern part of Africa, quite close to the equator, and right in the north there's a region called the Danakil Depression

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.430
which is well below sea level. It's part of the rift valley

00:00:54.949 --> 00:01:00.200
which is an area of huge seismic and volcanic activity.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.220
And in 2005, following an earthquake, a new lake appeared.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:15.280
I'm sure I don't pronounce it correctly, but its name looks to me as if it's pronounced Gaet'ale.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.729
What is really unusual is the water is very hot.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.530
60 degrees centigrade - that's hotter than you can bear to jump into. And it's hot because

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:34.149
there is a hot spring coming through the volcanic rock, and not only is it coming up and bubbling up

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:41.259
into the lake (you can see great bubbles coming up), but at the same time it is dissolving

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:47.580
salts that are in the soil. The concentration of the salt is so high,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.500
a mixture of calcium and magnesium chloride, that the water is actually

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.400
saturated. You cannot physically dissolve any more salt.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.799
There is 43.3%

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:05.400
dissolved solids by weight. What that means is if you have a liter of water,

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.509
you have four hundred and thirty-three grams of dissolved solid...

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.090
nearly half the weight of the water.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.530
Even in this small sample that I've got, you can see a big difference in weight.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:26.089
I've got an empty bottle, a bottle of water and a bottle of the water from the pond. We weigh the empty bottle, and

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.000
this balance has a thing that is called tare

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:36.320
which allows us to set it to zero when the bottle's on there, so we'll just weigh whatever's in the bottle.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.080
We put on...

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.240
the water...

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.660
comes to 15.5. We put this on here...

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.400
22.2.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:53.449
The other thing that you might notice is that because this water is at room temperature, and this room in

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.600
Nottingham is barely 20 degrees today,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.209
that some of the salt has precipitated.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:07.789
You can see that there are crystals at the bottom, and this is because, in general, these types of salts

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.989
dissolve better in hot water than they do in cold water.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.839
So when you put the water in here,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.810
let it cool down, some of the things precipitate. Some of you who know chemistry will say,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.170
"Calcium chloride and magnesium chloride are colorless,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:31.309
so why is this water yellow?" and the answer is that it contains a trace of

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:37.400
iron(III) which forms quite a strong yellow color in the presence of high

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:45.000
concentrations of chloride. So although it looks yellow, the yellow component is a really tiny part.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.299
Of course people have been looking for the saltiest water in the world for some time.

00:03:50.579 --> 00:03:52.110
This is a new record,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.499
and you might wonder what was the previous record... And the previous record was a pond

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.800
called the Don Juan Pond in the Antarctica

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.759
where there was a very - or there still is - a very salty lake which has

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.570
403 grams per liter of dissolved salt.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:19.039
Now, the reason why it's not as salty as this pond in Ethiopia is because

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:27.000
Ethiopia is near the equator, and the water is hotter than in Antarctica. As you cool down water,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.000
even though it's still saturated, it contains less salt, and the colder you go the less and less salt will dissolve.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.429
Eventually, when it freezes to ice,

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:43.939
you get no salt in it at all. You might ask, "How does this compare with the Dead Sea?"

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.429
I think there's about twice as much salt or dissolved material as the Dead Sea.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.519
According to Brady, the Dead Sea water

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.160
tastes like acid.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.480
("I would, but we haven't got much time.")

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.480
Ugh! [spits]

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.480
("Don't... yeah, don't... you didn't just taste it.")

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.900
Yeah, it's salty! [laughs]

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.900
We're not going to taste this, but broadly it will taste twice as awful

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.969
unless its taste is logarithmic in which case it might be a lot more.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.440
Compared to sea water it is about 14 times as salty.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.600
Well, 14 times as much dissolved salt.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.600
Sea water also has a different composisition: it has a lot of sodium chloride in it,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.739
whereas there's very little sodium in this, and the reason is that this is not

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.400
a pond where sea water has evaporated,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:50.200
but it's come from dissolved salts underground which do not originate from the sea.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.009
This is Eduardo's paper,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.000
published in the journal called Aquatic Geochemistry...

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.100
("Have you ever published in that one?")

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:02.700
No, because I'm not a geochemist, and I don't study water.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.000
("Is Eduardo?")

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:10.200
Eduardo is not, but he is a good chemist, and good chemists can do anything!

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:15.000
The chemical analysis of the water of the Gaet'ale Pond,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.600
a small water body located in the Danakil Depression, Ethipoia,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:29.000
resulted to be the most saline water body on Earth with total dissolved solids of 433 grams per kilogram.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.080
And when they...

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:40.219
started making these aerosols, in the aerosols they put a molecule rather like this,

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.550
which has carbon in the middle,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.800
three fluorine atoms, and a chlorine atom.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:07:00.309
This is the Akaki River. It's about 20 kilometers from the city centre, and we're right in the middle of...

